#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557354} :activity/id #uuid "9fc4ad01-5949-512d-b592-7603738c5b3b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051054 :topic/id #uuid "7d6f9e38-bd31-5a75-ae9b-e0749e0230ae" :topic/math-academy-id 1084} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b0c36c9-1238-5db2-9bf3-adf86360a2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-x+{x}^{3})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556489} {:answer/id #uuid "2d36f336-7f12-5ce4-89b8-7a8176903c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556526} {:answer/id #uuid "defa663b-3dc9-5a1c-ade3-ded5b3e7a26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(-x+{x}^{3})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556982} {:answer/id #uuid "eddea9b1-2c9d-555c-8db2-5118e8221975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557016} {:answer/id #uuid "f975fea1-88d6-5827-9969-fe173a2fbbee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556526} :answer-field/id #uuid "22867a70-1477-5193-a844-d0f5f136791c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1c69fb5-f0aa-5c39-a555-57a6bac7db2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139276" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a9db1a85c150c0bd6a8913a19387c7c717103cff1faf5266c7e21e774ab193ab.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=-{x}^{2}+2x+1$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x+1 & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ -2x(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0,$ and $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$  \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/87/8790ae667c9a2741e2a732ff69d9afe5633dc26df7b02eee52fb51478524282b.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x([-{x}^{2}+2x+1]-[{x}^{2}+1])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(2x-2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33505734-d9d3-5b43-ae80-e3a496f5ae08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8b33db-e437-5653-925f-1fa9cfe52297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556488} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4c00d2-5098-54c4-bfb3-fb1e4e39a687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556678} {:answer/id #uuid "991cbc71-8163-5cad-9451-b64c49988e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556798} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b5b62d-cb0a-5d58-aa38-44da5f8d23e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556898} {:answer/id #uuid "d3da23ec-8498-546a-b90f-b15aaef5d70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556678} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b8240cb-eb83-5b67-93ee-5d39ead36d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1d2ad0a0-cc2c-5c3a-a170-bcfe4847272e" :question/math-academy-id "q-11480" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y={x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are  $x=0$ and  $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={2\\pi (\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\text{ }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3fabfb31-ba4d-5286-ae69-a7ab6909a969" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557365}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a51d10-6bf8-56cf-a684-b246769cc4f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{162}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556476}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556476} :answer-field/id #uuid "3835d0c7-1e03-5584-bbd2-249f76f9390e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "64dd5dee-1a80-5aec-aff5-ee2daf8b8dbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-352256" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=x-3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(1-3x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}x(x-3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}({x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {3}^{3}}-\\frac{3}{4\\cdot {3}^{4}})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{324}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{162}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4178bcb0-dd69-588c-bb6e-e7bbae7e2e7c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017b1a76-531b-5999-856a-c2856cf6e3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556434} {:answer/id #uuid "53c6ef0e-1bb2-5ef7-9f88-d4cd6bc3f805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556621} {:answer/id #uuid "b2741a70-01bb-517c-9324-b10de8cb8cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556870} {:answer/id #uuid "bee7d01a-2656-5a41-aea3-236070d2cc02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556889} {:answer/id #uuid "d808c625-55b1-5cc5-86d8-f55b3ec150ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556889} :answer-field/id #uuid "becc477c-1df6-5756-8837-a73d10338340" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc5e2afa-3b34-5a5a-87d2-86576b259f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-11481" :question/problem "![](images/63/63bc2b3795556cd1f1b3b5b63c8d92816f64edcad364664db25acc0a7cc59b9e.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=3.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6edba391-8597-52e1-b1c5-ab751b3434bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cc3695-fd2f-5e9c-9272-a1e292466b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556542} {:answer/id #uuid "580870d0-e193-59a3-9434-1a8a76cd2f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556631} {:answer/id #uuid "769e4575-3ec0-5595-98e8-16bc2c2e7733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556697} {:answer/id #uuid "bca077a5-a543-519f-ae1e-c7640a8d2417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556888} {:answer/id #uuid "f98e642e-bbd1-51ea-8415-467094f109fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556542} :answer-field/id #uuid "f640bd4e-d214-5668-a628-34c871dc6526" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6fc2e8c7-fc0f-5be7-9a14-96326d528b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-139201" :question/problem "![](images/78/780120c106065874c04d97b8b6ef10e3e81e1cf9fd7c7b19bc633963cbadafe4.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=4x-2{x}^{2}$ and $y=2{x}^{3},$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=4x-2{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=2{x}^{3}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 4x-2{x}^{2} & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ 4x-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ -2x(-2+x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$  and $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1$ because we are only concerned about $x\\ge 0.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/e6/e695cfd83116df98b1530e2d53186de0ebcd03c6056c2b8d7c2272a76fbd790c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=2{x}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=4x-2{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x([4x-2{x}^{2}]-[2{x}^{3}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(4x-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8e1f6b4c-2b0c-5f76-b13b-1f43a0ca1120" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557349}}] :activity/title "The Shell Method: Rotation About the Y-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556416}]}
